hi I'm Nick Gillespie with Reason TV and  today we're talking with major Neill  Franklin retired from the Maryland State  Police he's also the executive director  of Law Enforcement Against Prohibition a  group of cops law enforcement people  active and retired who fight against the  drug war and its policies Neil thanks  for talking to us now thanks for having  me you guys have created a great study  that's online at leaps website and you  delivered a copy of this to Gil  Kerlikowske the current drug czar what  what are you trying to accomplish one of  the first things we wanted to do was put  together a comprehensive look at those  past four decades which we'll focus upon  financial cost human cost addiction Neil  you were a certain point in your career  you were a drug warrior and that changed  what what happened to flip you on this  issue when I retired in 1999 I went to  work for the Baltimore Police Department  and just a few months after my  retirement date I had a conversation  with a comrade of mine Edward totally he  was telling me about a case he was  working he was going to make one final  buy of cocaine from the mid-level drug  dealer he was working with the FBI on a  task force right here in DC and as we've  had in the past we've had these  conversations and we didn't you know we  weren't thinking about the inherent  danger or the possible danger it's  October 30th I got a phone call late one  night woke me up and it was a co-worker  saying that it totally had been shot  excuse me  so my wife and I get in to the car and  we drive down the PG in General Hospital  and it's too late what had happened when  the dealer it had driven the dealer to  the location to get their drugs the  dealer came back to the car supposedly  with the drugs and he pause for a moment  to extinguish the cigarette and soon as  he did that he reached in the car and  shot ed totally at point-blank range in  the head and it was that moment you know  that night and those weeks after and  those months after where I just said  what are we doing and I started thinking  about the other officers that I knew  that had died in just a couple of years  after that more officers in Baltimore  City were dying from drug crime you know  dealers the prohibition part and that's  when I made my turn  that's when I stumbled across leaps  website when looking for this  information in his research trying to  find others who think like I do what is  the best way to honor the death of  officer totally and people like it the  best way that we can honor them is to  completely dismantle this system of  prohibition and implement a new system  focused on treatment and education  period we know it we know that needs to  be done why don't we just do it what are  the big round numbers or the findings of  the war on drugs what what has it been  doing and what has it been how has it  been affecting American society when we  first started the war on drugs we only  had a few hundred thousand people in  prison today we're somewhere around 2.5  million people in prison over those 40  years we put over 39 million people in  prison and a vast majority of those are  from nonviolent drug offenses call that  that's things like simple possession  yeah  no level dealing right we're not talking  about kingdoms and even though we've  arrested a few we're looking at 1.3  trillion dollars spent on law  enforcement efforts we're talking about  cops judges judicial system in prisons  as quite costly when you look at the  number of bodies look at Mexico over the  past five years alone since we stepped  up efforts in assisting Mexico in  fighting back against the cartel you're  looking at over I think the number sound  like 38,000 people murdered at the hands  of the KOAT cartel in Venezuela it's  even more in our very own country in our  neighborhoods and in our communities  every year somewhere between five to  eight thousand people murdered at the  hands of a neighborhood gang  why do police chase push back against  the drug war as it's currently  constituted what's it doing to their  efforts to actually control crime you  have to think about the history of how  local police got involved in this in the  first place it came from the federal  government in the form of funding we  were and I sometimes I usual we bribed  into getting into this war many police  agencies now continued to balance their  budgets through federal funds through  grants firm grants and what have you and  and to lose that money would be  significant and you will hear some  police chief speak to that so and yet  they're against the war on drugs well  they say that it's failed that it's not  working  they just haven't taken the next step to  move away from it to really end it and  so that they continue they feel  pressured politically and sometimes you  can't blame them but because they care  about their jobs politically they're  pressured to toe the federal party line  what are the things that we can do  quickly and immediately to redress that  to start fixing it and get out of this  war mentality so immediately where you  don't need any legislative action  whatsoever the president can move  marijuana from a Schedule one category  down to a three or four and that would  mean I mean currently marijuana is  scheduled under drug class federal drug  issues was having no medical value  absolutely and then we that's already  been proven wrong with the with the 15  states that are now moving in Medical  Marijuana and with the many doctors  behind that but in doing that in  completely removing it from the schedule  system whatsoever then he gives the  authority then to the states to move and  to begin to develop models to manage  marijuana within their communities and  the next important thing is it's now  time for a discussion not a debate  that's been done the discussion as to  what are the best possible models how  can we manage these dangerous drugs  within our communities we already know  that prohibition does not work so now we  have to regulate and control let's have  your discussion come up with some  solutions we've also been down this road  before in the 70s you know we heard  stories that philip morris had  trademarked names like Acapulco gold and  Maui Wowi anticipating the legalization  of marijuana Jimmy Carter seemed to be  weeks away before a big scandal broke  and pulled back from legalizing drugs in  any way shape or form other countries  have decriminalized Portugal as  decriminalized drugs but are we actually  at a different stage of the discussion  over the drug war completely different  than back in the 70s for a number of  reasons number one we didn't have the  data that we have today prison numbers  financial numbers body count we didn't  have those numbers when you look at the  leaders from Mexico the former  presidents have come out with the  Commission just a couple weeks ago in  their report yeah we have Republican  people running for the Republican  presidential nomination Senator Ron Paul  absolutely but here's another  significant part they didn't have leap  they didn't have Law Enforcement Against  Prohibition back in the 70s and we're  making a significant difference because  we bring a completely different  perspective we've been on the frontlines  we've been out there in this battle and  we can speak from that perspective  letting people know that this is a  complete failure what is the best way  both for civilians and lawn  forcement to find out more about leap  well they can go to  cops say legalize drugs dot-com and we  have a brand new website up very  comprehensive with some very good  information and they really should visit  that site major Neill Franklin retired  Maryland State Police executive director  of leap Law Enforcement Against  Prohibition thanks very much for talking  to us thanks Nick I'm Nikhil SP for  Reason TV
 

